---
/**
 * hospitality / CtaBandBK — "Villa Marindu" CLOSING CTA BAND
 * (design element data-component="CTA-BAND").
 *
 * ROLE. The last ask on the page, and the only full-bleed photograph after the
 * hero. A wide evening picture fills the band edge to edge, the design's
 * LEFT-TO-RIGHT scrim lays over it, and a single 600px copy column sits on the
 * dark side of that scrim: brass eyebrow, the biggest Prata line after the h1,
 * one sentence, and exactly two ways to act — the owner's own CTA in brass and
 * the owner's own phone number in the outlined secondary.
 *
 * DESIGN VALUES, lifted verbatim from "Villa Marindu Landing.dc.html":
 *   section   position:relative; overflow:hidden; border-top 1px #262E2B  → --rule
 *   photo     absolute inset:0, z 0                          → .plate + .ph
 *   scrim     absolute inset:0, z 1, pointer-events:none,
 *             linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(11,14,13,.95) 0%,
 *                             rgba(11,14,13,.78) 54%,
 *                             rgba(11,14,13,.5) 100%)        → --scrim-rgb
 *   copy      relative z 2, max-width 1280 / 34px sides      → .wrap
 *             block padding 104px, column max-width 600px
 *   eyebrow   10px / .44em caps / #B99552 / 600              → .kick
 *   h2        Prata 400, clamp(34px,4.8vw,68px)/1.05,
 *             margin .22em 0 .32em, #F4F1EA                  → --bone-hi
 *   sub       17px / 1.75 / #C3C8C1, margin 0 0 32px, 44ch   → --grey-2
 *   actions   flex, gap 13px, wrap, align-items center
 *   primary   btnBrass — brass fill, #101413 label, 12px/.16em caps,
 *             17px 32px, 2px radius                          → .btn.btn--fill
 *   secondary 1px rgba(239,234,224,.4), #EFEAE0 label, same type,
 *             17px 30px, 2px radius                          → .btn.btn--line
 * Every one of those colours is a PageBK token (R1); there is not a literal hex
 * in this file. btnBrass is character-for-character the wrapper's .btn--fill and
 * the outlined action is character-for-character .btn--line apart from its 30px
 * side padding, so this file adds exactly one button declaration.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (every path below is declared in components/editor/
 * genericContentSchema.ts, so every one is live in the sidebar AND inline; a
 * data-field on any other path is a dead hook that focuses nothing):
 *   ctaBand.image      — data-image-field, the full-bleed evening photograph
 *   ctaBand.tag        — the eyebrow (.kick)
 *   ctaBand.headline   — the h2, set:html so an owner's <em> takes the
 *                        template's signature brass italic anywhere inside
 *                        their own sentence
 *   ctaBand.sub        — the one sentence under it
 *   ctaBand.cta.text + data-href-field ctaBand.cta.href — the brass action
 *   location.phone     — the outlined action (data-field ONLY, see THE PHONE)
 *
 * DERIVED, never stored and never editable:
 *   · the CTA's href, resolved through onPage() against the ids PageBK actually
 *     mounts, and its target/rel pair — one conditional each, so no attribute is
 *     ever emitted twice
 *   · the tel: href, built from the phone's own digits
 *   · whether the band draws a photograph and therefore a scrim at all
 *
 * NOT RENDERED, deliberately:
 *   · No copy of any kind is written by this file. The design's demo strings
 *     ("Ready when you are", "Send me your dates.") are the DESIGNER filling in
 *     their own template. A real business gets a closing band that says only
 *     what that business said, so every guard sits OUTSIDE its tag: no eyebrow
 *     over nothing, no empty action row, and no scrim painted over a photograph
 *     nobody uploaded.
 *   · The design's `pointer-events:none` on the copy stack with `pointer-events:
 *     auto` on the action row. That pair exists so the DESIGNER can click the
 *     image-slot lying behind the words. In the admin editor it would make the
 *     eyebrow, the headline and the sub unclickable — inline editing dead, text
 *     unselectable — so it is dropped, exactly as HeroBK drops it. Nothing sits
 *     behind the copy here that a visitor needs to reach.
 *   · The design's `margin-top:100px`. In the design every section carries top
 *     padding only; in this template PageBK gives every section top AND bottom
 *     padding, so keeping the margin would stack ~200px between the FAQ and this
 *     band. The design's own 104px of block padding is kept instead (same
 *     conversion HowBK makes for its 92px band).
 *   · .reveal. This is the last ask on the page; a scroll-reveal that never
 *     fires (no IntersectionObserver, a script error, a blocked bundle) would
 *     hide the one thing the band exists to do.
 *   · An id. This band is not an anchor target — PageBK mounts about, services,
 *     gallery, location, faq and main, and the design gives the CTA band none of
 *     them. '#book' resolves to '#main' through ANCHOR_ALIAS below.
 *   · An aria-label on the <section>. Naming it would make it a landmark
 *     announced with a word I wrote; the h2 already titles the region.
 *
 * SECTION GATE (R5). The band renders when it has BODY content: an action (the
 * CTA label or a phone number), the sub-line, or the photograph. It deliberately
 * does NOT count the eyebrow or the headline — lib/derive-content-defaults.ts
 * fills ctaBand.headline with "Ready when you are." on EVERY site, so a gate
 * that counted it would be a no-op that ships a headline with nothing under it.
 * derive also fills ctaBand.cta.text unconditionally, which is why the band does
 * in practice render on a stock build — but that is a real, working action
 * pointed at a real id on this page, not a decoration, and an owner who clears
 * it and supplies no phone, no sub and no photo drops the whole band from the
 * document rather than leaving a lone line of type.
 *
 * THE PHONE ANCHOR CARRIES data-field ONLY — no data-href-field. Its href is
 * derived from the field's own value, so a data-href-field there would write
 * "tel:+63…" back into the phone field on the first inline edit; V3 also bails
 * out of inline editing on tel:/mailto: anchors and on anything carrying
 * data-href-field. This is the family's single documented exception to "every
 * anchor gets both".
 *
 * THE PHONE'S READ CHAIN is content.location.phone || content.contact.phone,
 * which is exactly what the sidebar's Location → Phone input reads (the schema
 * declares location.phone with fallbackPaths ['contact.phone'] and writes back
 * to the primary), so the rendered number and the field this hook opens are
 * always the same value. layout.contact.phone — the display-formatted mirror the
 * builder derives — is deliberately NOT preferred: astro-builder formats
 * content.contact.phone and ignores location.phone entirely, so on a site whose
 * owner typed a number into Location it would render a DIFFERENT number from the
 * one the hook edits. Same chain as FaqBI and LocationBI.
 *
 * WHY A location.* HOOK IS SAFE HERE, and where its real edge is. editorBridge
 * resolves a Blocks toggle to the ONE section holding the most hooks of that
 * prefix (scored by weight, not by document order) and hides only that node. The
 * page's location.* census is LocationBK 4 (tag, headline, address, sub),
 * FooterBK up to 3 (phone, address, the colophon's derived address) and this
 * band 1 — so "hide LOCATION" resolves to Location, and it keeps resolving there
 * after the owner hides it: a live toggle only sets display:none, so Location's
 * four hooks stay in the DOM and keep winning.
 *
 * FOR REVIEW — the edge is NOT "this band becomes the only location.* node"; it
 * is a TIE, and a narrower one. On a build where visibility.locationBlock is
 * already false LocationBK is never emitted, and if that same owner has a phone
 * but NO address the footer drops to a single location.* hook and ties this
 * band's one. editorBridge compares with a strict `>`, so the FIRST host it
 * scored keeps the win — this band, which sits in <main> ahead of the footer —
 * and re-toggling Location would blank the closing band. Preview-only: the
 * published build re-renders from the visibility flags. The alternative
 * (dropping the hook, as FaqBI had to) costs the number its inline edit on every
 * site to spare that one, so the hook stays. Note the related hazard is not this
 * file's to fix: on the same build, an owner WITH an address makes FooterBK the
 * richest holder, and "hide LOCATION" blanks the footer whether or not this band
 * carries a hook at all.
 *
 * ACCESSIBILITY (R9). The photograph is aria-hidden. It is a scrimmed backdrop
 * carrying no information the copy beside it does not already give, and the only
 * honest name available to it is the business name the hero has already
 * announced; naming it would repeat that at the foot of the page for no gain.
 * The attribute is a literal here precisely because it is unconditional — there
 * is no second, conditional aria-* beside it to collide with. The scrim is
 * aria-hidden for the same reason. The tel: link's accessible name is the
 * owner's own number and nothing else — no "Call …" prefix I invented.
 *
 * CONTRAST. Over the scrim's left region the ground is effectively --night
 * (.95 at the left edge, ~.79 where the 600px column ends at 1280px), so the
 * design's measured ratios hold: --bone-hi headline 16.5:1, --grey-2 sub 11.1:1,
 * --accent eyebrow 6.62:1, --on-accent on the brass fill 6.62:1. See RESPONSIVE
 * SCRIM below for how that density is held once the column goes full-width.
 *
 * CLASS-NAME LAW (R4, not inferable, do not "tidy"): the section is .vm-close,
 * never .cta-band — buildOverrideCss pins `.cta-band .btn { color: <on-ink>
 * !important }` for every state and would strand both labels here. Buttons are
 * .btn + .btn--fill / .btn--line (double dash) for the same reason. Never
 * .btn-primary / .btn-ghost / .btn-yellow / .btn-light / .nav-cta / .trust /
 * .testi in this family.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content } = Astro.props;

// Anchor guard, copied from the HeroBK sibling and pointed at THIS page.
// lib/derive-content-defaults.ts hard-codes ctaBand.cta.href = '#visit', an id
// no page in this family renders, so without this the last button on the page
// would scroll nowhere on every first build. Resolve every in-page href against
// what PageBK actually draws: keep it when the id exists, remap it when this
// design carries the same block under another id, otherwise fall back to the
// target the design was built around. Off-page hrefs (tel:, mailto:, http) pass
// through untouched.
// Keep PAGE_ANCHORS and ANCHOR_ALIAS in step with PageBK — and with the copies
// in HeaderBK, HeroBK and FooterBK. There is no shared import by design (a
// section component in this repo receives only `content` + `layout`); the copies
// must stay identical by hand.
const PAGE_ANCHORS = new Set(['about', 'services', 'gallery', 'location', 'faq', 'main']);
const ANCHOR_ALIAS: Record<string, string> = {
  visit: 'location', contact: 'location',
  rooms: 'services', amenities: 'services',
  host: 'about', why: 'about',
  work: 'gallery',
  book: 'main',
};
const onPage = (href: string | undefined, fallback: string): string => {
  if (!href) return fallback;
  if (!href.startsWith('#')) return href;
  const alias = ANCHOR_ALIAS[href.slice(1)];
  if (PAGE_ANCHORS.has(href.slice(1))) return href;
  return alias && PAGE_ANCHORS.has(alias) ? `#${alias}` : fallback;
};

const cta = content.ctaBand || {};

// The HOOK below is always ctaBand.image, the path the sidebar's image picker
// writes. The quote escape keeps a filename with an apostrophe from closing the
// url('') early and killing the declaration.
const bandImage = String(cta.image || '');
const bandImageCss = bandImage.replace(/'/g, '%27');

const tag = cta.tag || '';
const headline = cta.headline || '';
const sub = cta.sub || '';

// Canonical path first, legacy shape second. The schema owns ctaBand.cta.text /
// .href; CtaBandBI emitted cta1/cta2/cta3 hooks that no field declares, so a
// site edited under that template can hold a label at ctaBand.cta1.text. It is
// read for DISPLAY ONLY — the hook is the canonical path in both cases, so the
// sidebar's CTA box wins from the next keystroke and the value heals onto the
// path the schema actually owns.
const ctaText = cta.cta?.text || cta.cta1?.text || '';
const ctaHref = onPage(cta.cta?.href || cta.cta1?.href, '#location');
// target="_blank" only for http(s): the design's demo CTA opened a messenger in
// a new tab, but an owner may point this at #location or a tel:, and hijacking
// those would be wrong. One conditional per attribute, so neither is duplicated.
const ctaExternal = /^https?:/i.test(ctaHref);

// See THE PHONE'S READ CHAIN in the doc comment.
const phone = content.location?.phone || content.contact?.phone || '';
const telHref = phone ? `tel:${String(phone).replace(/[^0-9+]/g, '')}` : '';

const hasActions = Boolean(ctaText || phone);
// SECTION GATE (R5) — body content only: an action, the sentence, or the photo.
// Neither the eyebrow nor the headline counts; see the doc comment.
const hasBand = Boolean(hasActions || sub || bandImage);
---
{hasBand && (
<section class="vm-close">
  {/* Layer 0 — the photograph. No upload ⇒ no layer at all, rather than a
      tinted rectangle standing in for a picture nobody supplied. */}
  {bandImage && (
    <div class="plate vm-close-shot">
      <div
        class="ph vm-close-ph"
        data-image-field="ctaBand.image"
        style={`background-image:url('${bandImageCss}');`}
        aria-hidden="true"
      ></div>
    </div>
  )}
  {/* Layer 1 — the design's left-to-right scrim, verbatim. Purely
      presentational, and it only exists where there is a photograph to
      darken. */}
  {bandImage && <div class="vm-close-scrim" aria-hidden="true"></div>}

  {/* Layer 2 — the copy column. */}
  <div class="wrap vm-close-wrap">
    <div class="vm-close-col">
      {tag && <span class="kick vm-close-kick" data-field="ctaBand.tag">{tag}</span>}
      {headline && <h2 class="h2 vm-close-h" data-field="ctaBand.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}
      {sub && <p class="vm-close-sub" data-field="ctaBand.sub">{sub}</p>}
      {hasActions && (
        <div class="vm-close-acts">
          {ctaText && (
            <a
              class="btn btn--fill"
              href={ctaHref}
              target={ctaExternal ? '_blank' : undefined}
              rel={ctaExternal ? 'noopener noreferrer' : undefined}
              data-field="ctaBand.cta.text"
              data-href-field="ctaBand.cta.href"
            >{ctaText}</a>
          )}
          {phone && (
            <a class="btn btn--line vm-close-tel" href={telHref} data-field="location.phone">{phone}</a>
          )}
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
)}
<style>
  /* Colour, type and metrics come only from PageBK's token vocabulary
     (--night --bone-hi --grey-2 --accent --rule --scrim-rgb --bone-rgb --disp)
     and from its shared primitives (.wrap .kick .h2 .plate .ph .btn .btn--fill
     .btn--line). There is no literal hex in this file.

     SPECIFICITY NOTE: PageBK wraps its primitives in :where(), so a scoped rule
     here scores (0,2,0) against the wrapper's (0,1,1) — including the wrapper's
     OWN media queries. Anything overridden below must therefore be restated at
     every breakpoint this file touches, which is why the headline size and the
     secondary button's side padding both reappear at 520px. */

  /* Design: position:relative; overflow:hidden; border-top 1px --rule; the copy
     layer's 104px of block padding, scaled on the shared curve so it never
     crushes on a phone. The design's margin-top:100px is dropped — see the doc
     comment. */
  .vm-close {
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    padding: clamp(64px, 8.5vw, 104px) 0;
  }

  /* The photograph fills the band edge to edge: no radius, no floor height, no
     reserved minimum — it is a ground, not a framed plate. */
  .vm-close-shot {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 0;
    min-height: 0;
    border-radius: 0;
  }
  /* Two classes deep on purpose: the wrapper's `:where(.plate) .ph` scores one
     element more than a single scoped class, so `.vm-close-ph` alone would lose
     to it. Restating cover/center costs nothing and means the photograph cannot
     letterbox if that primitive is ever narrowed. No colour is restated — that
     stays in the primitive, where the theme engine keeps control of it. */
  .vm-close-shot .vm-close-ph {
    background-size: cover;
    background-position: center;
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
  }

  /* The design's gradient, unchanged: heavy on the left where the column sits,
     opening to half over the picture's right side. rgba(11,14,13,…) is
     --scrim-rgb. */
  .vm-close-scrim {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 1;
    pointer-events: none;
    background: linear-gradient(
      90deg,
      rgba(var(--scrim-rgb), .95) 0%,
      rgba(var(--scrim-rgb), .78) 54%,
      rgba(var(--scrim-rgb), .5) 100%
    );
  }

  /* No width here — .wrap owns the measure, and a width on this class would
     out-specify it and blow the column out to the full viewport. */
  .vm-close-wrap {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 2;
  }
  /* Design: max-width 600px. min-width:0 so a long unbroken string inside it
     cannot widen the column. */
  .vm-close-col {
    max-width: 600px;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /* Design: Prata clamp(34px,4.8vw,68px)/1.05, margin .22em 0 .32em. The column
     above is the measure, so the shared .h2's 24ch ceiling is released. The
     brass italic <em> is the wrapper's own global rule and reaches set:html
     markup that a scoped rule could not. */
  .vm-close-h {
    font-size: clamp(34px, 4.8vw, 68px);
    line-height: 1.05;
    margin: .22em 0 .32em;
    max-width: none;
  }

  /* Design: 17px / 1.75 / --grey-2, margin 0 0 32px, max-width 44ch. Written out
     rather than borrowing .lede, which is a different colour, size, leading and
     measure — every value would have been overridden anyway. */
  .vm-close-sub {
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--grey-2);
    margin: 0 0 32px;
    max-width: 44ch;
  }

  /* Design: flex, gap 13px, wrap, align-items center. */
  .vm-close-acts {
    display: flex;
    gap: 13px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
  }
  /* Design: the outlined action is the shared button at 30px of side padding
     rather than 32px — the only metric in which it differs from .btn--line. */
  .vm-close-tel {
    padding-left: 30px;
    padding-right: 30px;
  }

  /* RESPONSIVE SCRIM. The design's gradient is tuned to a 600px column sitting
     in the left 47% of a 1280px band, where the scrim never falls below ~.79.
     Once the column goes full-width the gradient's .5 tail would land under the
     words, so the band holds the density the copy already had rather than
     re-lighting the picture. Same values, held further right — not a new
     treatment. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .vm-close-scrim {
      background: linear-gradient(
        90deg,
        rgba(var(--scrim-rgb), .95) 0%,
        rgba(var(--scrim-rgb), .80) 100%
      );
    }
    .vm-close-col { max-width: none; }
    .vm-close-h { font-size: clamp(30px, 5.8vw, 48px); }
    .vm-close-sub { font-size: 16.5px; }
  }

  /* Nothing may push the page sideways at 360px: long unbroken strings break
     anywhere, and a long uppercase label wraps inside a full-width button
     instead of widening the row. */
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .vm-close-kick {
      max-width: 100%;
      overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    }
    .vm-close-h {
      font-size: clamp(27px, 8.2vw, 36px);
      overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    }
    .vm-close-sub {
      font-size: 16px;
      max-width: none;
      overflow-wrap: anywhere;
      margin-bottom: 26px;
    }
    .vm-close-acts {
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: stretch;
      gap: 11px;
    }
    .vm-close-acts .btn {
      width: 100%;
      white-space: normal;
      text-align: center;
    }
    /* Re-join the wrapper's 520 pass: without this the secondary keeps its 30px
       sides while the primary drops to 24px. */
    .vm-close-tel {
      padding-left: 24px;
      padding-right: 24px;
    }
  }
</style>
